**What I did:** Fixed a classification flaw in `analytics/scripts/staleness-triage.js` (ZAZU-owned infra — fallback path a).

**The bug:** The CLOSE bucket ("likely moot") fired purely on `overdue > 14 days`. That mislabeled the **passport renewal** — Dad had *escalated it to HIGH, pulled the due date in to Oct 17, and edited the notes on Oct 12* (only 21 days ago). Recommending Dad CLOSE something he'd just personally prioritized is exactly backwards; it could clear wanted work off the board.

**The fix:** Added `lastHumanTouchDays()` (most recent non-ZAZU history entry — Zazu's own resistance/backfill writes don't count) and a `HUMAN_TOUCH_RECENT_DAYS = 30` guard. A wide-overdue task a human engaged within 30 days now routes to **CONFIRM** ("still wanted, just overdue — reschedule or knock it out") instead of CLOSE. Tasks with no human engagement (the financial-planning doc, created from Mom's request but never touched since) correctly stay CLOSE.

**Result:** CLOSE list 2→1; the one removed is precisely the one with active human intent. Regenerated `staleness-triage.md`, script runs clean.

**Why this and not another nudge/proposal:** I verified first — board data quality is clean (0 missing fields), the prior cycle's date-math fix is complete (the other scripts measure elapsed duration between two full timestamps, not the calendar-day-vs-now pattern, so no analogous bug), and Proposal 04 is already written and awaiting Dad. This was a concrete, non-duplicative correctness bug fully completable in one session.

